nk  all of them very tasty of course but  it's not just the tea  so all three places have very similar  political struggles  and activists in the places for um in  the case of thailand  and hong kong they have been  there had been some protests and the  protests have been  anti-authoritarian and all the activists  share the same goal they want  to have more democracy i love milk tea  too but it is a very strange  name how did this start it all started  earlier this year when a thai actor  retweeted a post  that referred to hong kong as a country  and then his girlfriend a taiwanese  girlfriend  also tweeted something about the  coronavirus that angered  chinese netizens so online users from  china or both  started to troll and flooded social  media platforms with pro-beijing posts  so to fight back some thai netizens  started to use  some memes very quick witted memes  with hashtags and one of them was mukti  alliance  and that quickly got a lot of echoes and  instantly  people from hong kong and taiwan had  been joining this movement  and posting uh created even more mims  and so it all started as a joke but then  it became a real bond among the  activists i mean how tangible  is this alliance so things are happening  on social media but to what extent  are protesters in hong kong and thailand  supporting each other  as we've seen in the report thai  protesters have been demonstrating for  hong kong at their very own protests  and in the meanwhile hong kong activists  have also  tried to help their counterparts um  over the weekend there had been an  online petition  at change.org that was initiated by thai  protesters  and the website has been instantly  blocked by the thai government  because it's sensitive and so after  reading the news hong kong  activists have campaigned online and  asked fellow hong kongers to help sign  the petition for  thailand and also signs uh like  stand with thailand refugees can be seen  in hong kong as well that's very  interesting to what extent  is there going to be an impact when we  look at these transnational protest  movements  i think it already has an impact so  through the multi alliance  there's been quite a lot of exchanges  among  thai and hong kong activists  and thai protests um in fact  thai protesters have uh adopted a lot of  strategies  uh from hong kong from the hong kong  protest last year that's why  when we compare the pictures of the two  protests we've seen that the pictures  are so similar that's the reason behind  it  and also i think a new tactic of protest  swapping  has emerged and it's unprecedented  because  it's two completely different places um  trying to promote each other's course  and that's now all possible um  because it's all online now and i think  this kind of borderless resistance  can really change the protest culture in  the coming years  that's very interesting and of course  you also see oregon protesters looking  online to see how hong kong protesters  have been fighting back against police  very interesting the globalization of  the protest  hangshan lee thank you  you

